Hi Andrew,

Thank you very much for your response.

Based on your response I specified the following model for the second-order factor:

model <- "F1 =~ HDL + TG + LDL + WC
F2 =~ HbA1c + SBP + WC

L1 =~ F1 + F2 + DEP_full
HDL ~~ a*HDL
TG ~~ b*TG
LDL ~~ c*LDL
WC ~~ d*WC
HbA1c ~~ f*HbA1c
SBP ~~ e*SBP
DEP_full ~~ g*DEP_full
F1 ~~ h*F1
F2 ~~ i*F2
a > .001
b > .001
c > .001
d > .001
e > .001
f > .001
g > .001
h > .001
i > .001
L1 ~ SNP"

Is this what you had in mind? Because when I run it, I still obtain the same output with NAs and negative residual variance within every single row. Are you aware of anything else that could be causing the error?

With regard to my second question, due to the presence of heterogeneity for some of the lead SNPs, I attempted to impose equality constraints on the factor loadings of all three traits. When I did that, I noticed that the CFI changed depending on whether I chose to use unit variance identification for the latent factor or constrained the loading of the first factor to be equal to 1. I thought that model fit indices should remain the same, regardless of whether we choose to set the std.lv <http://std.lv> parameter to TRUE or FALSE. Is the discrepancy in CFI values to be expected when imposing equality constraints? If so, which of the models (latent factor variance = 1 vs. factor loading = 1) is more appropriate for running a userGWAS? I have attached an .html file demonstrating the change in CFI values.

I appreciate your help and apologise for the number of questions.

Best wishes,

Vilte

